markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Understanding Boolean

#### Conditionals let your program make decisions. They check whether something is true or false and then choose what to do next.

<img src="https://images.code.org/1af7efc4c4b44eae9eb8969add99e2d4-image-1772738194332.png" alt="fork in the road" width="300px" style="display: block; float:right; margin: 40px 40px 40px 10px; border-radius:10px;">


## Think of it like a fork in the road

#### Imagine you’re walking and you reach a fork in the road.

#### There are only two possible paths:

- #### Go left

- #### Go right

#### You can’t go both ways at the same time. **You have to choose one.**

#### That’s exactly how a Boolean works in programming.


 <div style="clear:both;"></div>

 ---

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Boolean</strong> is a data type that can only have one of two values: true or false. 
</p>
</div>

#### In programming, Booleans are often the result of comparison operations (like age>18) and are used to control the flow of your program. 

---

## Next up

#### Let's see how booleans help build flowcharts.

